= Apamea, Syria

!!Apamea!! (Greek: Ἀπάμεια, //Apameia//; Arabic: أفامية,
[[ romanized | romanization-of-arabic ]]: //afāmiyah//) is an ancient
[[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]] and [[ Roman | ancient-rome ]] city
situated on the right bank of the [[ Orontes River ]], in the northwest of
modern [[ Syria ]]. Founded under the name !!Pella!! as a
[[ Macedonian | ancient-macedonians ]] military settlement following the
[[ Wars of the Diadochi ]], it was established as a fortified city around 300
BC by [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]], who renamed it after his wife, [[ Apama ]].
The city served as the capital of [[ Apamene ]] under the
[[ Seleucids | seleucid-empire ]][[ ^1 ]] and later became capital of the
[[ Roman province ]] of [[ Syria Secunda | roman-syria ]], a status it
retained into the [[ Crusader period | crusader-states ]].

Apamea came under Roman control in 64 BC when [[ Pompey ]] razed its fortress,
and it later withstood a three-year siege during the revolt of
[[ Quintus Caecilius Bassus ]]. The city suffered destruction in the
[[ Antioch earthquake | 115-antioch-earthquake ]] of 115 AD but was soon
rebuilt, with major reconstruction continuing through the second century. It
was destroyed again by [[ Khosrow I ]] in the sixth century and fell to
[[ Sasanian | sasanian-empire ]] forces during the
[[ Byzantine–Sasanian War | byzantinesasanian-war-of-572591 ]] before being
retaken. Following the [[ Muslim conquest of Syria ]], Apamea was partially
resettled and regained prominence under the [[ Hamdanid dynasty ]]; it later
passed to Crusader control under Tancred before being destroyed by an
earthquake in 1152.

The city's main remains include the Great Colonnade, an approximately 2 km
(1.2 mi) avenue lined with columns that constituted one of the longest such
structures in the [[ Roman world | roman-empire ]], and the Roman Theatre,
among the largest surviving
[[ theatres of the Roman Empire | roman-theatre-structure ]] with an estimated
[[ seating capacity ]] exceeding 20,000. The colonnade, rebuilt after the 115
AD earthquake and later modified under the [[ Byzantine | byzantine-empire ]]
emperor [[ Justinian I ]], formed the city's [[ cardo maximus | cardo ]] and
connected [[ baths | thermae ]], an [[ agora ]], and a [[ nymphaeum ]].
Additional features include a late antique [[ synagogue ]] with an inscribed
mosaic floor, later succeeded by a Christian
[[ church | church-architecture ]] on the same site, and the Great Hunting
Mosaic, a large early 5th-century floor [[ mosaic ]] recovered from a building
believed to have been the residence of the provincial governor.

Numerous artifacts from Apamea, including a reconstructed section of the Great
Colonnade and the Great Hunting Mosaic, are held at the
[[ Art & History Museum ]] in [[ Brussels ]]. Following the onset of the
[[ Syrian civil war ]], the site has sustained damage from looting.

== Geography

The site lies on the edge of the modern town of [[ Qalaat al-Madiq ]], about
55 km (34 mi) to the northwest of [[ Hama ]], [[ Syria ]], overlooking the
[[ Ghab valley ]].

== History

